## User

which would be best for getting all the permission  , flutter or java based

## Assistant

**Native Kotlin (Jetpack Compose) is the better choice for this permission set.** Not Flutter, and not Java unless you already know Java better than Kotlin.

### Why native wins here

Your demo hinges on **special-access Android APIs**, not normal runtime permissions:

| Need | API | Flutter reality |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Detect Instagram in foreground | `UsageStatsManager` | Thin/spotty plugins; usually a MethodChannel anyway |
| Block overlay | `TYPE_APPLICATION_OVERLAY` + `WindowManager` | Same — custom native code |
| Exact wake-up + full-screen alarm | `AlarmManager.setAlarmClock` + FSI | Alarm plugins rarely cover the full path |
| Persistent focus loop | FGS `specialUse` (Android 14+) | Manifest + service still written in Android |
| Boot restore | `RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED` | Native receiver |

Flutter can call all of these, but you still write Android platform code for the hard parts. For a 3-hour hackathon you pay Flutter’s overhead twice: Dart UI **and** Kotlin bridges.

### Kotlin vs Java

Same permission surface. Prefer **Kotlin + Compose** — shorter code, matches your existing plan, and is what current Android docs/samples use. Java only if the team is already faster in Java.

### Bottom line

- **Permissions / enforcement demo** → native Kotlin  
- **Flutter** → only if you need iOS later or already have a Flutter team; it does **not** make these permissions easier  

Your plan already picked the right stack. I’ll set up the Cloud Agent environment around the **Android SDK + JDK + Gradle / Kotlin** toolchain for that prototype unless you explicitly want Flutter instead.

